{
 "id": "2021-zhuang-flower",
 "name": "Flower Borders: Aesthetic Preference and Colour Attributes",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: participants rate the 18 flower-border photographs of the paper's Figure 1 (flower borders in Chinese urban green spaces), in random order and without a time limit, on the four statements of the paper's Table A1: \"This vegetation landscape is very beautiful.\" (aesthetic preference) and perceived colour contrast, colour brightness and visual richness, each on a 5-point scale from strongly disagree to strongly agree. Ends with gender and age. Analysis as in the paper: mean per photo, preference by attribute level (one-way ANOVA), regression of preference on the attributes, gender t-test. Original result (n = 113, Zhejiang University online survey): preference rose from about 1.7 to 4.1 across attribute levels; visual richness, contrast and brightness were the strongest predictors (adjusted R² = 0.44); men rated higher than women. Differences from the original study: (1) the photos are the small panels printed in Figure 1, upscaled, with the panel letters painted over, not the 3936 × 2214 px originals; (2) the lab part (facial-expression recording of valence and arousal, 8 s per photo after a 10 s white screen) and the six software-measured colour variables are not included; (3) the paper's Table 1 gives alternative verbal labels for the attribute points (e.g. very weak … very strong); this template uses the agreement anchors of Table A1 for all four items; (4) a \"Which describes you best?\" question (student / teacher / staff) is added in my own wording.",
